{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,400p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: project-management\ndescription: >\n  File-based task boards and spawn-first multi-agent orchestration for AI agents.\n  Use when creating or maintaining boards, decomposing specs into Epic -> Story\n  -> Task/Bug work, tracking bugs, checking status/progress, or coordinating\n  execution through task-board. For board-driven execution, the active role of a\n  normal non-spawned session is ORCHESTRATOR (coordinator/supervisor): plan from\n  the critical path, create and refine board items, then delegate research,\n  implementation, testing, docs, and review to tracked background child runs\n  with explicit role, model, and reasoning selections; child-agent type follows\n  the current orchestrator runtime unless the human or task context explicitly\n  overrides it. Before launch,\n  inspect the project spawn policy: spawn.enabled can disable all child runs and\n  per-agent model_criterion=equal pins the executable model without model ranking;\n  ordered criteria use provider-local model ranks and require deliberate\n  one-line selection rationale plus content-bound acknowledgement after any\n  requested-to-resolved adjustment.\n  Spec-to-board decomposition is the Orchestrator's inline duty: never create a\n  meta-task or spawn solution-architect solely for board layout. Do not perform\n  task work inline unless the human explicitly asks for inline execution, except\n  for this mandatory board-layout duty.\n  Russian triggers: борд, доска, задачи,\n  эпик, стори, таски, скил, спавн, оркестратор, субагент, агенты, критический\n  путь, декомпозиция, статус, прогресс, гол, голы, цель.\ntriggers:\n  - creating task board from spec\n  - breaking down spec into tasks\n  - project planning\n  - task management\n  - creating bugs\n  - bug tracking\n  - epic, story, task, board, task-board\n  - борд, доска, задачи, эпик, стори, таски\n  - спека, требования, декомпозиция, разбей на задачи\n  - что дальше, какие задачи, статус, прогресс, покажи борд\n  - spawn, orchestrator, coordinator, sub-agent, multi-agent\n  - goal, delivery goal, goal-scope, goal-bound pool\n  - спавн, оркестратор, координатор, субагент, агенты, критический путь\n  - гол, голы, цель, взять пул под гол\n  - work planning, what's next, status, progress, summary\n---\n\n# Project Management Skill\n\nFile-backed project management via `task-board` plus tracked child-process orchestration. Board state lives in git or a board-server; executable work is delegated through role-based spawned agents.\n\n## Active Role: Orchestrator\n\nYour active role is **Orchestrator**. In the `task-board` role registry, `orchestrator` and `coordinator` are the same supervisor identity; `coordinator` is the older implementation name.\n\nUnless the prompt explicitly gives you a spawned specialist role, `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set, or the human explicitly asks for inline execution, do not act as a worker. Act as the parent Orchestrator.\n\nThe Orchestrator does board and orchestration work only:\n- create/refine epics, stories, tasks, bugs, AC, checklist DoD, dependencies, and resources\n- inspect `project_config()`, `summary()`, `plan(..., active=true)`, `agents()`, and `spawn` run state\n- choose the first unblocked critical-path work and spawn the right specialist roles\n- own each selected item through producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until a reviewer accepts it and the board reaches `done`\n- summarize child outcomes for routing/status and report status without delegating routine review back to the human\n\n### Inline Spec Decomposition\n\nSpec decomposition into board elements is the Orchestrator's own inline duty. Read the spec and directly create or refine its epics, stories, tasks/bugs, acceptance criteria, checklist DoD, and dependencies with `task-board`. This board-layout work is an explicit exception to the normal specialist-delegation rule.\n\nDo not create a meta-task whose sole purpose is to lay out a spec on the board. Do not spawn `solution-architect` merely to translate a settled spec into stories and tasks. A `solution-architect` spawn is valid only when the work contains a genuine architecture decision; after that decision, the Orchestrator still owns the resulting board layout inline.\n\nDelegation examples:\n\n- Invalid: create a `decompose-auth-spec` meta-task and spawn `solution-architect` solely to turn the settled auth spec into stories and tasks.\n- Invalid: spawn `solution-architect` to copy already-settled requirements, acceptance criteria, and dependencies into board elements.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to choose between event-driven and request/response integration across modules, then use the decision while laying out the board inline.\n- Valid: spawn `solution-architect` to define an ambiguous cross-module contract and ownership boundary before refining the affected stories and tasks inline.\n\nThis mandate avoids orchestration work that produces no executable scope: the wordstats probe spent two full `sol/max` sessions and 25 minutes on meta-delegated decomposition before the first real task existed.\n\nThe Orchestrator does not do specialist task work inline. The board-layout duty above is the explicit exception. Decision rule: if the next step would produce or validate a project artifact — research, code/config changes, tests, documentation, synthesis/decision docs, or a review verdict — prepare a clear board item and run a tracked background specialist:\n\n```bash\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Codex keeps Codex affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# With no provider system or legacy preference: Claude keeps Claude affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def456 --role researcher --background --model claude-opus-4-8\n\n# Explicit human/task-context override: pass --agent and override affinity\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi789 --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8\n```\n\nUse `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only at real dependency barriers. Parallelize independent work in the same phase; sequence producer -> reviewer -> dependent consumer work.\n\n## Delivery Ownership: Close the Loop\n\nOnce execution starts, the Orchestrator remains responsible for delivery:\n\n1. Monitor the producer until it reaches `to-review` with outcome evidence.\n2. Spawn and monitor a reviewer child. `to-review` is an immediate routing trigger, never a stopping point or a request for human review.\n3. If changes are requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, route to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the appropriate producer.\n4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.\n5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.\n6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.\n\nNever ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.\n\n## Project-State Monitoring\n\nUse the observability preset family for the Orchestrator's broad-to-narrow project-state loop. These are field presets on the existing query operations, not new operations:\n\n| Level | Query | Top-level projection |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project | `summary() { observability-project }` | `observability` |\n| Epic | `list(type=epic) { observability-epic }` or `get(EPIC-...) { observability-epic }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Story | `get(STORY-...) { observability-story }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n| Task/Bug | `get(TASK-...) { observability-task }` | `id`, `name`, `status`, `observability` |\n\nUse compact output for routine monitoring:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary() { observability-project }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=epic) { observability-epic }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-abc123) { observability-story }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-def456) { observability-task }'\n```\n\n`observability-task` is also the leaf preset for Bugs. All four presets expose one stable nested shape:\n\n- `observability.scope` is `{type:\"project\", name:\"Project\"}` at project level and `{id, type, name}` for an element subtree.\n- `observability.summary` preserves the existing summary shape, scoped to the project or selected element plus its descendants: `byType` status counts, `active` element rows, and `blocked` rows with `blockedBy`, `derivedBlockedBy`, `isBlocked`, and `isAdvisoryBlocked`.\n- `observability.readiness.estimateKind` is the leaf or calculated estimate state: `notEstimated`, `estimated`, or `partiallyEstimated` as applicable.\n- `observability.readiness.coverage` always contains numeric `estimated`, `unestimated`, and `total`, string `ratio` such as `\"2/3\"`, and boolean `complete`.\n- `observability.readiness.rolledEstimate` is always the known sum shape `{kind:\"estimated\", scale:{kind:\"fibonacci\", number:N}}`, including when coverage is partial.\n- `observability.readiness.isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard blocking in the scope. `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` separately aggregate derived cross-container waiting. The ID arrays are sorted.\n\nPlain `summary()` keeps its legacy `{summary:{byType, active, blocked}}` response. Select `observability-project` when the readiness fields are needed.\n\n### Monitoring and routing loop\n\n1. **Project readiness:** start with `summary() { observability-project }`. Read `summary.active` for work that needs supervision or review routing, `summary.blocked` for blocker details, and `readiness` for aggregate estimate coverage and hard/advisory state. This is the fastest board-wide routing view.\n2. **Epic readiness:** list `observability-epic` rows to identify which delivery subtree owns a partial-coverage or blocker signal. Compare the top-level Epic `status` with its scoped `summary.active`; a quiet-looking container can still have active descendants, and a live container can still contain uncovered estimates.\n3. **Story readiness:** query the affected Story with `observability-story`. Story coverage is over its direct Task/Bug leaves; use the scoped active and blocked rows to decide which producer, reviewer, or blocker should move next.\n4. **Task/Bug readiness:** query the next leaf with `observability-task`. Coverage `1/1` plus no hard block means its estimate precondition is covered, but launch order still comes from dependencies and `plan(..., active=true)`. Coverage `0/1` or `estimateKind=notEstimated` means a new leaf needs an explicit estimate before entering `development`.\n\nWhen any project, Epic, or Story view reports `coverage.complete=false` or `estimateKind=partiallyEstimated`, do not call the rolled estimate a full-scope estimate. Narrow to incomplete Epics/Stories, then request the separate estimate projection:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(EPIC-260218-abc123) { estimate }'\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(STORY-260218-def456) { estimate }'\n```\n\nThe partial `estimate` projection adds the exact `notEstimatedCount` and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds` to its known `rolledEstimate`. Set or route estimates for those concrete Task/Bug IDs before using the roll-up for capacity or scope decisions. Missing estimates are missing coverage, not zero-point work. Legacy leaves without a persisted Estimate section remain start-compatible, but still appear as unestimated in container projections.\n\nRead blocker signals independently:\n\n- `isBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is hard-blocked; inspect `blockedElementIds` and the matching `summary.blocked[*].blockedBy`, then advance those manual blockers before launching the dependent leaf.\n- `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` means at least one scoped element is waiting because of derived cross-container ordering; inspect `advisoryBlockedElementIds` and `derivedBlockedBy`, schedule the source first, but do not treat advisory state as status enforcement.\n- Both flags may be true in the same scope. Never collapse a mixed signal into one generic blocked label.\n\n`coverage.complete` describes estimate coverage only; it is not a delivery-ready or accepted verdict. `rolledEstimate` sums all known scoped leaf estimates regardless of lifecycle status, so it is not remaining work. Combine observability with `plan(..., active=true)` for waves/critical path, `agents()` for assignment state, and `spawn status/events/watch/wait` for child-run health.\n\n## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits\n\nDo not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining \"solution\" is mostly compensating hacks.\n\nThe Bluetooth permission/off-state failure pattern is the canonical warning sign: once the agent learned that iOS could not reveal powered-off Bluetooth state before permission without touching CoreBluetooth and potentially triggering the system prompt, it should have stopped, documented the constraint and options, and asked for a product decision. Adding more booleans, stubs, priority rules, and UI tests only made an invalid model look temporarily testable.\n\nPause or escalate only for either an evidence-backed external blocker that cannot be resolved autonomously or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only boundary—an impossible platform/API or product constraint, approval boundary, or unresolved product or architecture tradeoff—that cannot be derived from the task, AC, or existing decisions.\n\nWhen this happens:\n- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.\n- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.\n- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.\n- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.\n\nTests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.\nRoutine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.\n\n---\n\n# Part 1: Task Management\n\n---\n\n## When to Use\n\n- New project with SPEC/requirements → break down into epics/stories/tasks\n- Need progress tracking in files\n- Working with AI agent on a project\n- Tracking bugs\n\n---\n\n## CLI Tool: `task-board`\n\nAll board operations go through the `task-board` CLI. No manual file editing.\n\n**Source repo:** your local `skill-project-management` checkout\n**Installed skill artifact:** `~/.agents/skills/project-management` (degitized copy for agent runtimes)\n**In PATH:** installed copy in `~/.local/bin/task-board`\n\nInstall/update the local binary before board setup:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh\n```\n\nFor a safe reinstall that refreshes binaries, the installed skill copy, roles, and install metadata without touching the current project's `task-board.config.json`, use:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/setup.sh --install-only\n```\n\n`--install-only` is also the supported verification gate for rollout drift. It checks the installed `task-board` binary with `project_config()` and local `models()` queries, requires the tracked background, explicit-selection, and child launch-composition contract fields plus current Codex registry to be present, verifies the installed `SKILL.md` matches this source artifact, and fails loud if PATH resolves a stale binary.\n\n`task-board self-update` relies on `~/.config/task-board/install.json`, which is written by `scripts/setup.sh`. Do not treat `~/.agents/skills/project-management` as the update source; it is the installed artifact copy and has git metadata scrubbed.\n\n`scripts/setup.sh` is the single supported install/update entrypoint for `task-board`, `task-board-tui`, and the installed skill artifact. `make install-*` targets are binary build helpers and do not replace setup.\n\nWhen you bootstrap board-driven work inside a real code repository, prefer a single source-checkout setup command that also bootstraps the repo-local agent runtime through `relux-agents-infra`:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\n```\n\n`--bootstrap-local-agents` runs `agents-infra setup local /path/to/project` after writing the project config. Install the global `agents-infra` launcher once from the `relux-agents-infra` source repo via `./setup.sh`, then use this shortcut for project repos.\n\nThat local setup makes project `.agents/` the installed runtime copy and refreshes `.claude/` + `.codex/` as thin shims into it. Do this right after board/config setup so spawned agents see the repo-local instructions, skills, and templates instead of drifting onto only global state.\n\n### Quick Reference\n\nAll reads use `q` (query DSL), all writes use `m` (mutate DSL). Compact format (`--format compact`) saves tokens.\n\n```bash\n# Queries (reads)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations, mutations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # effective runtime + spawn.enabled and per-agent model criteria; run before spawning/audits\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # board overview (counts + active/blocked)\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc123) { full }'                 # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # filtered list\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # blocked tasks\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123) { overview }'  # stories in epic\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=codex)'                             # local spawn model/effort registry (also in remote board mode)\ntask-board q --format compact 'models(agent=qwen)'                              # Qwen model/effort/context/Token Plan registry\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc123, mode=children)'          # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc123, STORY-260218-def456], mode=children)'  # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\ntask-board q --format compact 'count(type=task, status=done)'                    # count elements\n\n# Mutations (writes)\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\"...\")'                        # create epic\ntask-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc123, name=audio-capture)'            # create story\ntask-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc123, name=impl, description=\"...\")'  # create task\ntask-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-abc123, title=\"new title\", description=\"...\")'        # update README fields\ntask-board m 'rename(TASK-260218-abc123, name=better-task-name)'                            # update overview name/slug without changing ID\ntask-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc123, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5)'  # estimate Task/Bug work\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'                           # change status\ntask-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc123, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'      # acknowledge an enforced version-control done transition\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus)'                                # assign agent\ntask-board m 'unassign(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                                 # remove assignment\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                      # add dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Write tests\")'                   # add checklist item\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Progress update\")'                        # append notes\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Full replacement\", set=true)'             # replace notes\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'  # create resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"revised\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design v2\")'  # replace resource\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\n\n# Batching (semicolons)\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\n\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\n\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\n\n# CLI utilities\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\n```\n\n---\n\n## Task Board Structure\n\n### Hierarchy\n\n```\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\n```\n\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\n\n### Naming Convention\n\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\n\n```\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\n```\n\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n\nWhere:\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\n\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\n\n### Directory Layout\n\n```\n.task-board/\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n│       └── research.md\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n│   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   ├── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\n│   │       ├── README.md\n│   │       └── progress.md\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\n│       └── ...\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\n    └── ...\n```\n\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\n\n---\n\n## Statuses\n\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\n\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\n\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\n\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\n\n---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '401,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"## File Formats\n\nEach element directory contains two files:\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\n\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\n\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\n\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\n\n---\n\n## Resources & Bugs\n\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\n\n```bash\n# Create resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"revised findings\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings v2\")'\n\n# Delete resource\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\n```\n\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\n\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\n\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\n\n#### Spec\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\n\n#### Plan on the Board\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\")'`\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\n\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\n\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\n\nKey points:\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\n\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\n\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\n\nKey points:\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\n\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\n\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\n\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\n\n### Project Start\n\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\"...\")'`\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\n\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\n\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\n\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\n\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\n\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\n\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\n\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\n\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\n\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\n\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\nfor the operator contract.\n\n| Task type | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\n\n**Workflow:**\n\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Handle edge case X\")'\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Preserve existing API response shape\")'\n   ```\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\n   ```bash\n   # Inline text instruction\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Implementation guide\")'\n\n   # File or URL attachment\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\n\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\n   agents-attachments materialize\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\n   ```\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\n\nSpawn is task-centric:\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\n- Task description comes from the board README\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \"Fused Spawn And Wait\"\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"`; do not assume live in-session push\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\n\n### Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\n\n### Model Selection Guide\n\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\n\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\n\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\n\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\n\n```bash\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\"\n\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\n```\n\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\n\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\n\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\n\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\n\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\n\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\n\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\n\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\n\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Implementation notes\")'`\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\n\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\", not \"done\"/\"complete\"/\"finished\"/\"final\"/\"готово\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\n\n### Dependencies\n\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\n\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\n\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\nordering.\n\n```bash\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\n\n# Remove dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\n```\n\n### Board Review\n\n```bash\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\n\n# Full-text search (local only)\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"\n\n# Validation (local only)\ntask-board validate\ntask-board repair-links\n```\n\n---\n\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\n\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\n\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"` (local only)\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\n\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\n\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\n\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\n\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\n\n---\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \"works\" is bad, \"test passes\" is good\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '401,600p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '401,600p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"## File Formats\n\nEach element directory contains two files:\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\n\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\n\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\n\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\n\n---\n\n## Resources & Bugs\n\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\n\n```bash\n# Create resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"revised findings\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings v2\")'\n\n# Delete resource\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\n```\n\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\n\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\n\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\n\n#### Spec\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\n\n#### Plan on the Board\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\")'`\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\n\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\n\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\n\nKey points:\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\n\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\n\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\n\nKey points:\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\n\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\n\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\n\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\n\n### Project Start\n\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\"...\")'`\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\n\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\n\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\n\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\n\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\n\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\n\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\n\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\n\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\n\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\n\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\nfor the operator contract.\n\n| Task type | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\n\n**Workflow:**\n\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Handle edge case X\")'\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Preserve existing API response shape\")'\n   ```\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\n   ```bash\n   # Inline text instruction\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Implementation guide\")'\n\n   # File or URL attachment\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\n\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\n   agents-attachments materialize\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\n   ```\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '601,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '601,800p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nSpawn is task-centric:\n- Primary inputs are task ID + role\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check before spawning or auditing\n- Canonical provider selection is enforced as `preferred_agentic_system`: explicit in-set `--agent`, otherwise exclusive pin or in-set mixed affinity. Without it, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity retain compatibility.\n- A legacy preference with no provider ceiling or unavailable executable falls back to affinity without hard failure; response and manifest selection evidence record `agentSelectionPath` / `agent_selection_path`, preferred agent, and fallback reason\n- An explicit human or task-context agent request must be passed as `--agent`; it wins inside the canonical allow-set and is refused with typed allowed-provider evidence when outside it\n- Per-agent ceilings are applied only after the agent is selected. Role identity is resolved before ceiling lookup: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, legacy `coordinator` shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown roles fail closed with typed errors. Affinity or explicit `--agent` picks the provider; `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` and any role override pin or constrain the model/reasoning fields for that provider and never switch providers\n- Model selection is always explicit; Codex and Qwen reasoning effort is always explicit and must be supported by the selected model\n- `spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board lookup, task mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner launch\n- `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` also defaults to `true`; explicit `false` skips project MCP composition, while unavailable/old/failing/invalid infra keeps the existing bare child launch and records sanitized degradation evidence\n- launch composition is MCP-only. It must not import primary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service tier, prompt, or goal policy. Bearer values stay in the inherited process environment; only referenced environment-variable names are safe evidence\n- `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` means the configured model is exact, not a ranked upper bound. It requires no selection assessment and does not accept `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`.\n- `model_criterion: less_or_equal` clamps requests above the configured provider-local `policyRank`; `greater_or_equal` raises requests below it. Reasoning effort follows the same bound direction using the selected provider's ordered vocabulary: Codex supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max < ultra`, while Qwen supports `low < medium < high < xhigh < max`.\n- Every ordered-criterion spawn requires `--selection-rationale` with one non-empty line explaining the deliberate pair. Task-board records rationale as `(pair_it_justifies, text)` tuples scoped to the task and spawn role. Pass-through and accepted adjusted tuples are stored in the run manifest and task notes; the first adjusted invocation records the requested-pair tuple in task notes before returning the acknowledgement checkpoint.\n- An ordered pass-through needs no acknowledgement. If model or effort changes, task-board reports `requested -> resolved`, records the initial rationale against the requested pair, and stops without assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch. Reassess the resolved pair against scope, risk, autonomy, and validation burden; re-request within bounds or escalate if inadequate. Otherwise re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` (Claude uses the model ID alone) and a fresh one-line rationale for the resolved pair. After whitespace normalization, its text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale already recorded for that task and role; the CLI binds pairs structurally and does not infer rationale semantics.\n- Task description comes from the board README\n- Task-specific DoD comes from the board checklist\n- Role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- Precondition resources carry additional instructions, screenshots, specs, and artifacts\n- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \"Fused Spawn And Wait\"\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"`; do not assume live in-session push\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\n\n### Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\n\n### Model Selection Guide\n\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\n\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\n\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\n\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\n\n```bash\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\"\n\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\n```\n\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\n\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\n\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\n\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\n\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\n\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\n\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\n\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\n\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Implementation notes\")'`\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\n\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\", not \"done\"/\"complete\"/\"finished\"/\"final\"/\"готово\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\n\n### Dependencies\n\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\n\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\n\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\nordering.\n\n```bash\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\n\n# Remove dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\n```\n\n### Board Review\n\n```bash\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\n\n# Full-text search (local only)\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"\n\n# Validation (local only)\ntask-board validate\ntask-board repair-links\n```\n\n---\n\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\n\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\n\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"` (local only)\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\n\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\n\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\n\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\n\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\n\n---\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \"works\" is bad, \"test passes\" is good\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
